## 1. Hospital flow, same-day emergency care and admission avoidance

### Why this qualifies

Winter crowding directly affects patient safety, dignity, infection exposure and staff safety. Emergency department crowding is associated with delays, poorer patient experience and adverse outcomes, although the size of the causal effect varies between studies. Same-day emergency care is particularly relevant because it can reduce unnecessary overnight admission for patients who need urgent assessment but not inpatient monitoring. The NHS Urgent and Emergency Care Recovery Plan explicitly supports same-day emergency care and improved patient flow (NHS England, 2023).

### Interventions

| Action | Level and mechanism | Resources and requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **1.1 Expand consultant-led same-day emergency care with explicit inclusion and exclusion criteria** | **Organisational/clinical.** Rapid diagnostics, senior decision-making and ambulatory treatment avoid unnecessary admission. | **Medium–high:** senior clinicians, diagnostics, treatment space and evening/weekend cover. Requires agreed pathways with acute medicine, emergency medicine, radiology and community services. | **Moderate evidence.** Supported by observational evaluations and NHS implementation experience; comparative evidence on mortality and total system cost remains less certain. Usually feasible within **1–3 years**, but workforce is a major constraint. | Same-day emergency care conversion rate; admission avoidance; 4-, 12- and 24-hour waits; reattendance within 7 and 30 days; patient-reported dignity and safety. |
| **1.2 Introduce real-time whole-hospital flow management and senior escalation before crowding becomes critical** | **Organisational.** Makes bed capacity, delayed discharges, staffing and demand visible and enables earlier action. | **Medium:** operational team, reliable data and authority to coordinate wards, diagnostics, discharge and community partners. | **Moderate/mixed.** Flow dashboards and escalation systems are widely used, but evidence that dashboards alone improve outcomes is limited. **1–3 years.** Barrier: data quality and “gaming” of time measures. | Time from decision to admit to bed; occupancy; ambulance handover time; patients treated in non-clinical areas; delayed transfers; staff-reported workload. |
| **1.3 Establish senior-led alternatives to admission, including urgent community response and hospital-based treatment-at-home pathways** | **Organisational/system.** Provides assessment, diagnostics and treatment without exposing suitable patients to hospital harms. | **High:** multidisciplinary community teams, prescribing, diagnostics, transport and 24-hour escalation. | **Moderate evidence**, with stronger support for selected hospital-at-home models than for undifferentiated admission avoidance. **1–5 years.** Barrier: insufficient community capacity and unclear clinical responsibility. | Avoided admissions; 7- and 30-day readmission; mortality; adverse events; carer burden; patient preference; total pathway cost. |

**Classification:** 1.1 and 1.3 are **promising with moderate evidence**; 1.2 is useful operationally but is **not, by itself, strongly evidence-based**.

