## 2. Safe discharge, intermediate care and hospital-at-home

### Why this qualifies

As a patient or carer, “discharge” can feel like being transferred from a visible hospital system to an invisible burden at home. Winter pressure is often displaced into families, domiciliary care and community services. Early supported discharge and hospital-at-home can be safe for selected patients, but only when care, equipment, medication and escalation arrangements are genuinely available.

### Interventions

| Action | Level and mechanism | Resources and requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **2.1 Use a multidisciplinary discharge process beginning at admission, with a named coordinator and estimated discharge date** | **Clinical/organisational.** Identifies functional, housing, medication and carer requirements early. | **Medium:** discharge coordinator, therapy, nursing, social care and pharmacy involvement. **1–3 years.** | **Moderate evidence.** Early discharge planning is recommended internationally, but the effect depends on downstream capacity. Barrier: social care shortages and unrealistic discharge dates. | Percentage with discharge plan within 48 hours; discharge before midday; medication and equipment ready; readmission; carer-reported preparedness. |
| **2.2 Provide multidisciplinary intermediate care or hospital-at-home for clinically suitable patients** | **Organisational/system.** Substitutes home-based treatment for inpatient care while maintaining clinical review and escalation. | **High:** nurses, therapists, doctors, equipment, diagnostics, transport and 24-hour advice. | **Moderate evidence.** Cochrane evidence supports hospital-at-home for selected groups, with possible lower costs and similar outcomes, but not all patients are suitable (Gonçalves-Bradley et al., 2017). **1–5 years.** | Proportion discharged to supported care; mortality; readmission; falls; functional status; patient/carer experience; cost per episode. |
| **2.3 Give every patient a structured discharge bundle: medicines, red-flag symptoms, follow-up, transport and a contact number** | **Clinical/organisational.** Reduces uncertainty and enables earlier response to deterioration. | **Low–medium:** standardised checklist, pharmacist/nurse time, accessible written and verbal information. **1–3 years.** | **Moderate/mixed.** Individual discharge components are supported, but bundled interventions vary. Barrier: information overload and inaccessible language. | Medication discrepancies; follow-up completed; avoidable calls or reattendance; patient understanding; complaints relating to discharge. |

**Classification:** 2.2 is **evidence-supported but context-sensitive**. 2.3 is **high-yield and undervalued**, because many systems measure discharge speed rather than whether discharge was understood and manageable.

