## 3. Frailty and comprehensive geriatric assessment

### Why this qualifies

Older people are disproportionately affected by winter admissions, delirium, falls, deconditioning and loss of independence. A technically successful admission can still produce major harm if a person becomes unable to walk, eat, communicate or manage at home.

### Interventions

| Action | Level and mechanism | Resources and requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **3.1 Provide comprehensive geriatric assessment (CGA) in the emergency department, acute assessment unit and wards** | **Clinical/organisational.** Integrates medical, functional, psychological, medication and social assessment into one plan. | **Medium–high:** geriatrician or trained senior clinician, nursing, therapy, pharmacy and social care. **1–3 years** in hospitals with existing teams. | **Strong evidence** for CGA in suitable older inpatients; Cochrane review found benefits for being alive and living at home (Ellis et al., 2017). | Discharge home; functional status; delirium; falls; length of stay; readmission; mortality; patient goals achieved. |
| **3.2 Use a rapid frailty response pathway for people identified with frailty in emergency care** | **Clinical/organisational.** Ensures frailty-specific assessment, medication review, mobility and discharge planning rather than default admission. | **Medium:** screening, pathway, therapy and geriatric review. **1–3 years.** | **Moderate evidence**, often from observational and implementation studies. Barrier: frailty screening can become a tick-box exercise. | Time to frailty assessment; CGA completion; admission rate; mobility at discharge; delirium and falls. |
| **3.3 Make mobility, nutrition, hydration and delirium prevention part of every frailty care plan** | **Clinical.** Preserves function and reduces avoidable hospital-associated harm. | **Low–medium:** staff time, food assistance, mobility aids and environmental adjustments. **1–3 years.** | **Moderate evidence for component interventions**, but mixed for broad care bundles. Barrier: competing acute workload. | Daily mobility; nutritional screening and intake; delirium incidence; falls; pressure ulcers; functional decline. |

**Classification:** CGA is **strong evidence**. The wider frailty bundle is **moderate and implementation-dependent**.

