## 5. Respiratory infection prevention and seasonal vaccination

### Why this qualifies

Winter respiratory infection affects patients, carers and staff simultaneously. Avoiding infection is safer than trying to manage a surge after it has overwhelmed hospitals. Patients also need confidence that waiting rooms and wards will not expose them to avoidable infection.

### Interventions

| Action | Level and mechanism | Resources and requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **5.1 Deliver proactive influenza, COVID-19 and other nationally recommended vaccinations to patients and healthcare workers** | **Policy/organisational/clinical.** Reduces severe infection, staff absence and potentially transmission. | **Low–medium:** vaccine supply, outreach, convenient appointments, paid staff time and accurate registers. **Within 1–3 years.** | **Strong evidence for vaccination reducing severe disease**, while the effect of healthcare-worker influenza vaccination on patient outcomes is more uncertain (Ahmed et al., 2016). | Uptake by risk group and staff group; hospitalisation; staff sickness; vaccine equity; adverse events. |
| **5.2 Use layered infection prevention: ventilation, respiratory etiquette, hand hygiene, appropriate masks during high transmission and rapid isolation/cohorting** | **Policy/organisational/clinical.** Reduces exposure through multiple partially independent barriers. | **Medium–high:** ventilation assessment, estates work, PPE, isolation rooms and supplies. **1–5 years**, longer for estates upgrades. | **Strong evidence for some components, particularly hand hygiene and selected PPE; evidence for individual measures varies.** Barrier: overcrowding and old buildings. | Healthcare-associated respiratory infections; ventilation readings; hand-hygiene compliance; PPE availability; staff exposure incidents. |
| **5.3 Separate symptomatic patients from vulnerable patients in waiting and treatment areas, with rapid testing where it changes management** | **Organisational/clinical.** Reduces cross-infection and speeds appropriate treatment or isolation. | **Medium:** space redesign, triage staff and validated testing. **1–3 years.** | **Moderate evidence**, with testing effectiveness dependent on sensitivity, timing and whether results alter action. | Time from arrival to isolation; nosocomial infection; waiting-room density; test turnaround; patient-reported safety. |

**Classification:** Vaccination and basic infection prevention are **strong or moderate evidence**. Waiting-room redesign is **important and undervalued**, but comparative winter evidence is limited.

