## 6. Healthcare worker safety, staffing and retention

### Why this qualifies

Patients notice when staff are exhausted, rushed or frightened. Unsafe staffing creates longer waits, poorer communication, missed care and increased turnover. Staff safety is therefore a patient-safety intervention, not a competing priority.

### Interventions

| Action | Level and mechanism | Resources and requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **6.1 Implement evidence-informed staffing escalation plans, including minimum safe skill mix and surge staffing** | **Policy/organisational.** Reduces excessive workload and missed care. | **High:** substantive recruitment, bank staff, flexible rostering and workforce planning. **1–5 years.** | **Moderate–strong observational evidence** links nurse staffing and skill mix with patient outcomes, but exact staffing thresholds are context-dependent. | Registered nurse hours per patient day; agency dependence; missed care; falls; medication errors; sickness and turnover. |
| **6.2 Provide protected rest, psychological support, violence prevention and rapid occupational-health access** | **Organisational.** Reduces burnout, sickness absence and unsafe performance. | **Low–medium** for rest and occupational health; **medium** for security and environmental changes. **1–3 years.** | **Moderate evidence.** Organisational interventions generally outperform individual resilience training alone (West et al., 2016; Panagioti et al., 2018). | Sickness absence; burnout; violence incidents; staff retention; safety culture; patient complaints about communication. |
| **6.3 Give frontline staff authority and simple routes to escalate unsafe crowding, infection risk or staffing** | **Organisational/policy.** Converts latent safety concerns into early operational action. | **Low–medium:** standard escalation protocol, psychological safety and leadership response. **Within 1–3 years.** | **Promising/undervalued.** Evidence for speaking-up systems is less robust than evidence for safety culture generally. Barrier: fear of blame or retaliation. | Escalations received and acted on; time to response; staff survey psychological safety; incidents during escalation periods. |

**Classification:** Organisational action is more evidence-based than generic wellbeing apps or resilience courses. Workforce expansion is essential but **resource-intensive and unlikely to resolve within one winter**.

