## 7. Medication safety and antimicrobial stewardship

### Why this qualifies

Winter increases prescribing, transitions of care and use of antibiotics. Older patients and people with multimorbidity are particularly vulnerable to omissions, duplication, interactions and confusion after discharge.

### Interventions

| Action | Level and mechanism | Resources and requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **7.1 Conduct pharmacist-led medication reconciliation at admission and discharge for high-risk patients** | **Clinical/organisational.** Identifies discrepancies and ensures that the patient and next clinician share the same medication list. | **Medium:** pharmacists or trained pharmacy technicians, access to records and time for patient/carer discussion. **1–3 years.** | **Strong evidence for reducing medication discrepancies; effects on readmission and mortality are more variable** (Mekonnen et al., 2016). | Reconciliation completed; discrepancies identified and resolved; high-risk medicine errors; readmissions; patient understanding. |
| **7.2 Use prospective audit, feedback and decision support for hospital antibiotic prescribing** | **Clinical/organisational.** Improves antibiotic selection, duration and review while limiting resistance and adverse effects. | **Medium:** antimicrobial pharmacist/microbiologist, prescribing data and clinical review. **1–3 years.** | **Strong evidence** that audit and feedback and restrictive/supportive interventions improve prescribing (Davey et al., 2017). | Antibiotic days of therapy; guideline compliance; 48–72-hour review; C. difficile; resistance; mortality and readmission. |
| **7.3 Provide a single medicines information and escalation route across hospital, GP, pharmacy and care homes** | **Organisational/system.** Reduces contradictory advice and enables rapid resolution of discrepancies. | **Medium–high:** interoperable records, shared protocols and pharmacy capacity. **1–5 years.** | **Moderate/mixed**, because technical interoperability does not automatically improve clinical communication. | Unresolved medication queries; discharge discrepancies; pharmacy-to-GP communication; medicine-related reattendance. |

**Classification:** Antibiotic stewardship is **strong evidence**. Medication reconciliation is **strong for process outcomes**, with more limited evidence for major clinical outcomes.

