## 8. Interoperable information and closed-loop referrals

### Why this qualifies

From a patient perspective, repeating the same history to several services is not merely inconvenient. It can lead to missed results, duplicated tests, unsafe medicines and referrals that disappear into a queue.

### Interventions

| Action | Level and mechanism | Resources and requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **8.1 Implement interoperable shared care records with role-based access and visible data provenance** | **Policy/organisational.** Allows clinicians to see diagnoses, medicines, allergies, tests, care preferences and recent contacts. | **High:** digital infrastructure, standards, information governance, training and maintenance. **1–5 years**, longer for full interoperability. | **Moderate/mixed.** Interoperability improves information availability, but evidence for mortality or admission reduction is limited. Barrier: vendor incompatibility and privacy concerns. | Percentage of encounters with usable medication/allergy data; duplicate tests; clinician time searching; information-related incidents. |
| **8.2 Require closed-loop referrals: named recipient, acknowledgement, tracking, outcome and communication to patient and referrer** | **Organisational/clinical.** Prevents referrals being lost and makes responsibility explicit. | **Medium:** referral management team, digital tracking and agreed maximum response times. **1–3 years.** | **Moderate evidence from safety and implementation literature; direct winter trial evidence is limited.** | Referral acknowledgement; overdue referrals; time to first appointment; rejected referrals; patients informed of outcome. |
| **8.3 Use structured electronic handover at admission, transfer and discharge, including uncertainty and escalation plans** | **Clinical/organisational.** Preserves critical information during transitions. | **Low–medium:** standard template, training and protected handover time. **1–3 years.** | **Moderate evidence** for structured handover improving information transfer; clinical outcome evidence varies. | Handover completeness; omitted critical information; adverse events after transfer; patient/carer involvement. |

**Classification:** Closed-loop referrals are **high-yield and undervalued**. A shared record is **important but highly context-sensitive** and should not be treated as a technology-only solution.

