## 9. Equity-focused support for vulnerable patients and carers

### Why this qualifies

Winter pressure is not experienced equally. People in deprived areas, people experiencing homelessness, disabled people, people with limited English, rural communities, care-home residents and unpaid carers can face longer waits, greater exposure and more difficulty accessing alternatives.

### Interventions

| Action | Level and mechanism | Resources and requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **9.1 Use population risk registers to offer proactive winter support to high-risk patients** | **Policy/organisational/clinical.** Identifies people before deterioration and offers vaccination, medication review, care planning and escalation advice. | **Medium:** linked data, primary-care teams, community outreach and consent processes. **1–3 years.** | **Moderate evidence** for targeted preventive care; effects depend on accurate risk identification and capacity to act. | Uptake by deprivation, ethnicity, disability and age; emergency admissions; vaccination; care-plan completion. |
| **9.2 Provide accessible communication: interpreters, easy-read information, hearing support, translated safety-netting and non-digital routes** | **Clinical/organisational.** Enables informed decisions and reduces unsafe misunderstanding. | **Low–medium:** interpreter contracts, accessible formats and staff time. **Within 1–3 years.** | **Moderate evidence and strong ethical/practical rationale**, although outcome trials are limited. Barrier: interpreting delays and inadequate commissioning. | Use of interpreters; complaints about communication; understanding scores; missed appointments; outcomes by language and disability. |
| **9.3 Offer carer assessment, respite or practical support as part of discharge and urgent-care planning** | **Organisational/policy.** Prevents the NHS from transferring unrecognised clinical work and risk to an exhausted carer. | **Medium–high:** social care, voluntary-sector partners, respite and carer support. **1–5 years.** | **Moderate evidence for carer support improving wellbeing; evidence for reducing admissions is less consistent.** | Carer-reported preparedness; carer strain; delayed discharge; crisis contacts; unplanned readmission. |

**Classification:** Accessible communication and carer involvement are **undervalued** because they are poorly represented in hospital performance metrics. Outcomes must be stratified; an overall average can conceal widening inequality.

