## 10. High-value, low-carbon respiratory and urgent care

### Why this qualifies

Winter creates avoidable travel, repeated investigations, unnecessary admissions, excessive antibiotic prescribing and high energy demand. Sustainable care should not mean withholding treatment; it means providing effective care with less waste and fewer avoidable journeys.

### Interventions

| Action | Level and mechanism | Resources and requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **10.1 Implement evidence-based respiratory antimicrobial stewardship, including delayed or no antibiotics where clinically safe** | **Clinical/policy.** Reduces unnecessary antibiotic exposure, adverse effects and resistance. | **Low–medium:** clinician education, prescribing feedback and patient safety-netting. **1–3 years.** | **Strong evidence** for stewardship approaches in appropriate respiratory infections (Davey et al., 2017; NICE guidance). Barrier: diagnostic uncertainty and fear of missing deterioration. | Antibiotic prescribing; duration; reconsultation; complications; antimicrobial resistance; patient understanding. |
| **10.2 Develop clinically governed remote monitoring or virtual wards for carefully selected patients** | **Organisational/clinical.** Enables earlier discharge or home management while monitoring physiological deterioration. | **Medium–high:** devices, broadband or telephone alternatives, trained staff and 24-hour escalation. **1–5 years.** | **Moderate/mixed.** Virtual wards expanded rapidly, but evidence is heterogeneous and may not demonstrate benefit for every condition. Digital exclusion is a serious risk. | Admissions avoided; mortality; escalation; device adherence; equity of access; patient/carer workload; carbon per episode. |
| **10.3 Measure and reduce low-value tests, duplicated appointments and avoidable transport** | **Policy/organisational/clinical.** Reduces waste, exposure, emissions and patient burden without reducing necessary care. | **Low–medium** for audit and pathway redesign; **high** where transport or estates change is needed. **1–5 years.** | **Promising/undervalued.** Evidence for individual Choosing Wisely-type interventions varies; carbon and patient-burden outcomes are often not measured. | Duplicate tests; unnecessary follow-ups; travel distance; emissions per pathway; patient time and out-of-pocket cost; safety-netting failures. |

**Classification:** Antimicrobial stewardship is **strong evidence**. Virtual wards and carbon reduction are **promising but highly context-sensitive**.

